---The evaluation metrics for the find matches algorithm. The quality of your machine learning transform is measured by getting your transform to predict some matches and comparing the results to known matches from the same dataset. The quality metrics are based on a subset of your data, so they are not precise.
---@class aws.glue.FindMatchesMetrics
---@field AreaUnderPRCurve? aws.glue.GenericBoundedDouble The area under the precision/recall curve (AUPRC) is a single number measuring the overall quality of the transform, that is independent of the choice made for precision vs. recall. Higher values indicate that you have a more attractive precision vs. recall tradeoff.
---@field Precision? aws.glue.GenericBoundedDouble The precision metric indicates when often your transform is correct when it predicts a match. Specifically, it measures how well the transform finds true positives from the total true positives possible.
---@field Recall? aws.glue.GenericBoundedDouble The recall metric indicates that for an actual match, how often your transform predicts the match. Specifically, it measures how well the transform finds true positives from the total records in the source data.
---@field F1? aws.glue.GenericBoundedDouble The maximum F1 metric indicates the transform's accuracy between 0 and 1, where 1 is the best accuracy.
---@field ConfusionMatrix? aws.glue.ConfusionMatrix The confusion matrix shows you what your transform is predicting accurately and what types of errors it is making.
---@field ColumnImportances? aws.glue.ColumnImportanceList A list of ColumnImportance structures containing column importance metrics, sorted in order of descending importance.

---The parameters to configure the find matches transform.
---@class aws.glue.FindMatchesParameters
---@field PrimaryKeyColumnName? aws.glue.ColumnNameString The name of a column that uniquely identifies rows in the source table. Used to help identify matching records.
---@field PrecisionRecallTradeoff? aws.glue.GenericBoundedDouble The value selected when tuning your transform for a balance between precision and recall. A value of 0.5 means no preference; a value of 1.0 means a bias purely for precision, and a value of 0.0 means a bias for recall. Because this is a tradeoff, choosing values close to 1.0 means very low recall, and choosing values close to 0.0 results in very low precision.
---@field AccuracyCostTradeoff? aws.glue.GenericBoundedDouble The value that is selected when tuning your transform for a balance between accuracy and cost. A value of 0.5 means that the system balances accuracy and cost concerns. A value of 1.0 means a bias purely for accuracy, which typically results in a higher cost, sometimes substantially higher. A value of 0.0 means a bias purely for cost, which results in a less accurate FindMatches transform, sometim...
---@field EnforceProvidedLabels? aws.glue.NullableBoolean The value to switch on or off to force the output to match the provided labels from users. If the value is True, the find matches transform forces the output to match the provided labels. The results override the normal conflation results. If the value is False, the find matches transform does not ensure all the labels provided are respected, and the results rely on the trained model.

---Specifies configuration properties for a Find Matches task run.
---@class aws.glue.FindMatchesTaskRunProperties
---@field JobId? aws.glue.HashString The job ID for the Find Matches task run.
---@field JobName? aws.glue.NameString The name assigned to the job for the Find Matches task run.
---@field JobRunId? aws.glue.HashString The job run ID for the Find Matches task run.

