---Additional options for streaming.
---@class aws.glue.KafkaStreamingSourceOptions
---@field BootstrapServers? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty A list of bootstrap server URLs, for example, as b-1.vpc-test-2.o4q88o.c6.kafka.us-east-1.amazonaws.com:9094. This option must be specified in the API call or defined in the table metadata in the Data Catalog.
---@field SecurityProtocol? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty The protocol used to communicate with brokers. The possible values are "SSL" or "PLAINTEXT".
---@field ConnectionName? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty The name of the connection.
---@field TopicName? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty The topic name as specified in Apache Kafka. You must specify at least one of "topicName", "assign" or "subscribePattern".
---@field Assign? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty The specific TopicPartitions to consume. You must specify at least one of "topicName", "assign" or "subscribePattern".
---@field SubscribePattern? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty A Java regex string that identifies the topic list to subscribe to. You must specify at least one of "topicName", "assign" or "subscribePattern".
---@field Classification? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty An optional classification.
---@field Delimiter? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty Specifies the delimiter character.
---@field StartingOffsets? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty The starting position in the Kafka topic to read data from. The possible values are "earliest" or "latest". The default value is "latest".
---@field EndingOffsets? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty The end point when a batch query is ended. Possible values are either "latest" or a JSON string that specifies an ending offset for each TopicPartition.
---@field PollTimeoutMs? aws.glue.BoxedNonNegativeLong The timeout in milliseconds to poll data from Kafka in Spark job executors. The default value is 512.
---@field NumRetries? aws.glue.BoxedNonNegativeInt The number of times to retry before failing to fetch Kafka offsets. The default value is 3.
---@field RetryIntervalMs? aws.glue.BoxedNonNegativeLong The time in milliseconds to wait before retrying to fetch Kafka offsets. The default value is 10.
---@field MaxOffsetsPerTrigger? aws.glue.BoxedNonNegativeLong The rate limit on the maximum number of offsets that are processed per trigger interval. The specified total number of offsets is proportionally split across topicPartitions of different volumes. The default value is null, which means that the consumer reads all offsets until the known latest offset.
---@field MinPartitions? aws.glue.BoxedNonNegativeInt The desired minimum number of partitions to read from Kafka. The default value is null, which means that the number of spark partitions is equal to the number of Kafka partitions.
---@field IncludeHeaders? aws.glue.BoxedBoolean Whether to include the Kafka headers. When the option is set to "true", the data output will contain an additional column named "glue_streaming_kafka_headers" with type Array[Struct(key: String, value: String)]. The default value is "false". This option is available in Glue version 3.0 or later only.
---@field AddRecordTimestamp? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty When this option is set to 'true', the data output will contain an additional column named "__src_timestamp" that indicates the time when the corresponding record received by the topic. The default value is 'false'. This option is supported in Glue version 4.0 or later.
---@field EmitConsumerLagMetrics? aws.glue.EnclosedInStringProperty When this option is set to 'true', for each batch, it will emit the metrics for the duration between the oldest record received by the topic and the time it arrives in Glue to CloudWatch. The metric's name is "glue.driver.streaming.maxConsumerLagInMs". The default value is 'false'. This option is supported in Glue version 4.0 or later.
---@field StartingTimestamp? aws.glue.Iso8601DateTime The timestamp of the record in the Kafka topic to start reading data from. The possible values are a timestamp string in UTC format of the pattern yyyy-mm-ddTHH:MM:SSZ (where Z represents a UTC timezone offset with a +/-. For example: "2023-04-04T08:00:00+08:00").

