---A structure for a machine learning transform.
---@class aws.glue.MLTransform
---@field TransformId? aws.glue.HashString The unique transform ID that is generated for the machine learning transform. The ID is guaranteed to be unique and does not change.
---@field Name? aws.glue.NameString A user-defined name for the machine learning transform. Names are not guaranteed unique and can be changed at any time.
---@field Description? aws.glue.DescriptionString A user-defined, long-form description text for the machine learning transform. Descriptions are not guaranteed to be unique and can be changed at any time.
---@field Status? aws.glue.TransformStatusType The current status of the machine learning transform.
---@field CreatedOn? aws.glue.Timestamp A timestamp. The time and date that this machine learning transform was created.
---@field LastModifiedOn? aws.glue.Timestamp A timestamp. The last point in time when this machine learning transform was modified.
---@field InputRecordTables? aws.glue.GlueTables A list of Glue table definitions used by the transform.
---@field Parameters? aws.glue.TransformParameters A TransformParameters object. You can use parameters to tune (customize) the behavior of the machine learning transform by specifying what data it learns from and your preference on various tradeoffs (such as precious vs. recall, or accuracy vs. cost).
---@field EvaluationMetrics? aws.glue.EvaluationMetrics An EvaluationMetrics object. Evaluation metrics provide an estimate of the quality of your machine learning transform.
---@field LabelCount aws.glue.LabelCount A count identifier for the labeling files generated by Glue for this transform. As you create a better transform, you can iteratively download, label, and upload the labeling file.
---@field Schema? aws.glue.TransformSchema A map of key-value pairs representing the columns and data types that this transform can run against. Has an upper bound of 100 columns.
---@field Role? aws.glue.RoleString The name or Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the IAM role with the required permissions. The required permissions include both Glue service role permissions to Glue resources, and Amazon S3 permissions required by the transform.
---@field GlueVersion? aws.glue.GlueVersionString This value determines which version of Glue this machine learning transform is compatible with. Glue 1.0 is recommended for most customers. If the value is not set, the Glue compatibility defaults to Glue 0.9. For more information, see Glue Versions in the developer guide.
---@field MaxCapacity? aws.glue.NullableDouble The number of Glue data processing units (DPUs) that are allocated to task runs for this transform. You can allocate from 2 to 100 DPUs; the default is 10. A DPU is a relative measure of processing power that consists of 4 vCPUs of compute capacity and 16 GB of memory. For more information, see the Glue pricing page.
---@field WorkerType? aws.glue.WorkerType The type of predefined worker that is allocated when a task of this transform runs. Accepts a value of Standard, G.1X, or G.2X.
---@field NumberOfWorkers? aws.glue.NullableInteger The number of workers of a defined workerType that are allocated when a task of the transform runs.
---@field Timeout? aws.glue.Timeout The timeout in minutes of the machine learning transform.
---@field MaxRetries? aws.glue.NullableInteger The maximum number of times to retry after an MLTaskRun of the machine learning transform fails.
---@field TransformEncryption? aws.glue.TransformEncryption The encryption-at-rest settings of the transform that apply to accessing user data. Machine learning transforms can access user data encrypted in Amazon S3 using KMS.

